dec={
'175736093':('CONFIRMED','target-supported','Gaia-assisted-consistent',True,'Two bounded raw eclipses at BTJD2568.765270 and3794.018769 recover ~30ppt depths. The conditional47-cycle clock is26.06922338d. Raw-only integer aliases survive the limited sectors46/91. Exhaustive positive-integer cycle bounds using the source Gaia period leave only47cycles within3sigma; nearest alternatives differ25.7/28.2sigma. Full covariance conditioning on the measured clock leaves the same-conjunction epoch consistent (two-sided tail~0.152). Independent S91 custom aperture recovers2.82% loss with target-centered deficit0.047pixel away.','Obtain a third complete same-kind eclipse to resolve the clock independently of Gaia; retain Gaia-assisted dependence and conditional timing error.'),
'187242860':('REFUTED','off-target-neighbor','not-associated',False,'Repeated ~9ppt minima follow a0.78114d cadence (conservative double-minimum guide1.562282d), unrelated to the48.45044d Gaia orbit. S35 difference peak(6,3) lies on Gaia5635500054501795712 at(6.218,3.284), while target is(3.894,3.712). Target-centered3x3 aperture recovers only0.26% loss. The short signal disappears in S99; a clean covered prediction at4066.30471 has only1.13ppt depth. Spatial ownership, rather than the sector-dependent depth alone, refutes a target eclipsing claim.','The neighboring eclipser may be characterized separately; no new target EB claim or target by-catch is established.'),
'281540788':('PROBABLE','target-supported','rejected-paired-conjunction-geometry',True,'Repeated deep eclipses are real. Six same-kind raw midpoints give40.75489255d at cycles0,1,45,63,69,70 with maximum residual0.00167d. The opposite kind occurs12.70980875d later and is never mixed into the clock. Source Gaia period40.83924877d differs2.57sigma, but its eccentric conjunction pair is inconsistent: observed12.70981d versus full-covariance MC median17.89037d and99.73% interval14.33053–20.42365d (two-sided tail2.08e-5). Epoch cancellation makes this stronger than individually propagated late conjunction windows. S1 primary and secondary kinds are independently imaged from the same cutout: aperture losses14.5%/15.2%, target-centered offsets0.132/0.156pixel, both peaks at target pixel(4,3). This establishes both kinds on target at TESS resolution. Physical probable EB is retained with failed Gaia geometry; association to the published Gaia orbit is excluded.','Obtain a revised RV orbit or independent spectroscopy; keep both eclipse kinds spatially checked and do not report Gaia geometry as passed.'),
'298092295':('REFUTED','no-established-eclipse-source','not-established',False,'The selected S14 SAP depressions at~1690.4/1694.5 sit in structured drift and smooth changing minima, without a secure discrete same-kind eclipse train. Local fitted trough depths8–10ppt are descriptive fits, not established eclipses; KSPSAP attenuates corresponding core contrasts to2.3/5.0ppt. The wider raw context includes strong rising ramps and resets. Seven other extracted sectors show no secure matching raw eclipse morphology. Completed review refutes the selected eclipse interpretation, while making no exhaustive all-sector nondetection or on-target variability claim.','Any future claim requires resolved bounded raw eclipses, demonstrated recurrence and source attribution.'),
'298976044':('CONFIRMED','target-supported','Gaia-assisted-consistent',True,'Two clear bounded rounded raw eclipses at1732.901356 and3567.462422 have~100ppt fitted depths. The conditional36-cycle clock is50.96002962d. Raw-only aliases survive coverage in sectors15/16/56/83; no newer sector was found. Source Gaia period50.96024820d uniquely admits36positive integer cycles within3sigma (neighboring aliases>44sigma away). After full covariance conditioning on the measured period, the same-conjunction epoch remains consistent at a two-sided tail~0.072. S83 independent custom aperture recovers8.62% loss and a target-centered deficit0.086pixel away.','Obtain a third same-kind eclipse for a Gaia-independent clock; preserve the conditional period error and explicitly Gaia-assisted inference.'),
'308091169':('REFUTED','known-neighbor-contamination','not-associated',False,'Both selected events belong to the known nearby Prsa EB TIC308091162 / Gaia4580706767219118464, catalog period3.35457022d and separation51.38arcsec. Ten S79/80 midpoints yield a local3.35452605d short clock. S26 and S79 difference peaks(2,2)/(4,6) track the known neighbor positions(2.303,1.548)/(4.297,5.880); target positions are(3.972,3.341)/(3.008,3.748). Target3x3 losses are only0.04%/0.17%. The S26 positive near-target centroid is undefined because the actual deficit is outside the restricted weighting region; it is not quoted as an astrometric measurement. Pixel peak/neighbor positions in two orientations are decisive. The long extrapolation phase discrepancy does not establish a separate target event. Gaia25.49655d is unrelated to the short train.','No new target EB claim; retain the known neighbor identity and do not assign its catalogue classification to the target.'),
'403192875':('CONFIRMED','target-supported','consistent',True,'Three raw midpoints at3176.370105/4146.486119/4182.415728 give35.93021098d at cycles0/27/28 with maximum residual0.00031d. S68/104 have complete bounded23/22ppt eclipses; S102 ingress is interrupted, so it supplies a midpoint only after shifting the initial search guide to the measured trough. Third-event timing and covered-null tests remove other tested integer/divisor aliases. Gaia period differs0.80sigma and the same conjunction is within~1sigma of source covariance predictions. S68 custom aperture recovers2.17% eclipse and target-centered deficit0.164pixel away. The VSX RS entry is generic non-eclipsing variability and does not establish a prior EB identification.','More complete events can improve the ephemeris; retain cadence and spatial-model systematic limits.')}
